    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0369" n="301" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg301"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72112/1399½: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Windsor, Vt.</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-27T15:45:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 27, 1915—3:45 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Instructions of Sir Edward Grey to Ambassador about cotton gives me deep
                        concern.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d259-f1"/> Am I mistaken in my recollection
                    that the British Government formally notified us that cotton would be regarded
                    as not already contraband and would be continued to be so treated?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodrow Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d259-f1" n="82" xml:id="d259-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 2510, July 22, 1915, from the Ambassador in Great Britain, <ref
                        target="frus1915Supp#pg_193">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1915, supp., p.
                    193</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
